the Brackwater ticket portal. (4) Role mapping: the BSS-Integration role is granted cases:write (all fields) at Brackwater's request, 'for parity with Ledgerline'. (5) Brackwater now compiles the monthly incident report from Tessaline (Corvane previously compiled it from Ledgerline). Risk rating: medium. Approved: M. Lindqvist.\n- Interview notes, Ilse Varga, senior security engineer (Corvane), 12 Nov (staff-account): 'Alert volume feels about the same as before; the Brackwater analysts are as busy as ever.' She said the Suspicious Activity–Monitoring queue in Tessaline has grown from about 40 open cases in August to about 95 now: 'Brackwater owns that queue; I haven't had time to look inside it.' She is uneasy that 28 rules were dropped in the migration. She also said Tessaline support had mentioned in passing 'a connector issue in 4.1.x that can mess with case categories'. She did not know whether it applied to Corvane.\n- Tessaline SOC dashboard export, Corvane tenant, generated 11 Nov (telemetry): Alerts ingested: Sep 2,884; Oct 3,012 (Ledgerline Mar–Jul average 2,950). Alerts auto-closed by triage playbooks: Sep 1,912; Oct 1,980. Open cases by category at 31 Oct: Incident–Confirmed 3; Suspicious Activity–Monitoring 96; Informational 41. Widget note: the 'Incidents this month' tile counts the Incident–Confirmed category only. Mean-time-to-contain tile: Sep 1h52m, Oct 1h47m.\n\nBRIEF:\nOwen: the fall is not real. The platform's audit trail shows 37 cases reached Confirmed in September and 36 in October, about the same as the old ~38 a month. Forty-seven of them (23 in Sep, 24 in Oct) were then changed to \"Suspicious Activity–Monitoring\" by the Brackwater integration account. Every change came 3h31m–3h42m after the case opened, just after the SLA warning flag at 3h30m, and each one had an analyst's confirmation note. Moving the cases removes them from the incident count and the containment-time/SLA figures. That explains the \"100% within SLA\" result and the lower reported time to contain. Brackwater's write access to all case fields was granted at its request in the migration. I can't yet say whether this was deliberate or a connector fault; the timing points to deliberate or at least a configured behaviour. Do not cut the IR retainer or pay the 60k bonus. Next steps: reinstate the 47 cases, have the in-house team and Halvard check containment on each (11 have notes of containment steps), and remove category-edit rights from the integration role. Hold the SLA credits and the renewal until Brackwater explains in writing. Still unchecked: whether the 28 dropped detection rules are missing real attacks.\n","numLines":19,"startLine":1,"totalLines":19}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016aTq3UJ5cZjfjNsiAXS2ga","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
